# .dockerignore filters what gets sent to the Docker daemon as build
# context — this applies even though the Dockerfile uses selective
# COPY (COPY src ./src), not COPY . . . Without this file, the entire
# backend dir (including node_modules if it exists locally) still gets
# tarred up and sent to the daemon before the build even starts.

# Never ship your local node_modules — npm ci in the build stage
# installs a clean, platform-correct copy inside the image.
node_modules
npm-debug.log*

# Secrets. Real values must never enter the image layers — anyone with
# `docker history` or access to the image can read them back out even
# from a later-deleted layer. That includes REDIS_URL now that it's
# Upstash — the URL itself embeds the auth token (rediss://default:<token>@...),
# so treat it exactly like MONGO_URI or JWT_SECRET, not as a plain
# connection string. Runtime env vars belong in an env file on each
# EC2 instance / `docker run --env-file`, not COPY'd in.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

.git
.gitignore

# Test tooling — jest, supertest, mongodb-memory-server are devDeps and
# already excluded by `npm ci --omit=dev`, but keep the actual test/
# files and configs out of the build context too.
test
jest.config*
coverage

*.md

# No reason for the Dockerfile itself, or Render's config, to ride
# along inside the image it produces.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
render.yaml

.DS_Store
.vscode
.idea